      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "Strix",
          "informationUri": "https://strix.ai",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-89",
              "name": "SQL Injection in /rest/products/search?q Parameter",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "SQL Injection in /rest/products/search?q Parameter"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The application is vulnerable to SQL injection via the q parameter in the /rest/products/search endpoint. User-supplied input is concatenated directly into a SQL LIKE query without parameterization, allowing an attacker to inject boolean-based SQL conditions to extract all products from the database or manipulate query results."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The application is vulnerable to SQL injection via the q parameter in the /rest/products/search endpoint. User-supplied input is concatenated directly into a SQL LIKE query without parameterization, allowing an attacker to inject boolean-based SQL conditions to extract all products from the database or manipulate query results.\n\nAn attacker can extract the complete product database through boolean-based blind SQL injection. By manipulating the q parameter with SQL payloads like `' OR '1'='1`, all 46 products are returned regardless of search query. This allows unauthorized access to product data including names, prices, descriptions, and image references. With further exploitation, database schema enumeration is possible via SQLite's sqlite_master table. The vulnerability could potentially be extended to access other database tables if the application's database permissions are not properly restricted.\n\nReplace the vulnerable string concatenation with parameterized queries using prepared statements. In Node.js with SQLite, use placeholders (?) for all user input. In the vulnerable query `SELECT * FROM products WHERE name LIKE '%' + userInput + '%'`, change to `SELECT * FROM products WHERE name LIKE ?` and pass `['%' + userInput + '%']` as a separate parameter array. Alternatively, use an ORM (Sequelize, TypeORM, Prisma) that handles parameterization automatically. The key principle is that SQL structure and user data must be submitted to the database engine separately, never combined as a string. Add input validation as defense-in-depth (whitelist allowed characters, limit length), but this is not a substitute for parameterization.",
                "markdown": "The application is vulnerable to SQL injection via the q parameter in the /rest/products/search endpoint. User-supplied input is concatenated directly into a SQL LIKE query without parameterization, allowing an attacker to inject boolean-based SQL conditions to extract all products from the database or manipulate query results.\n\nAn attacker can extract the complete product database through boolean-based blind SQL injection. By manipulating the q parameter with SQL payloads like `' OR '1'='1`, all 46 products are returned regardless of search query. This allows unauthorized access to product data including names, prices, descriptions, and image references. With further exploitation, database schema enumeration is possible via SQLite's sqlite_master table. The vulnerability could potentially be extended to access other database tables if the application's database permissions are not properly restricted.\n\nReplace the vulnerable string concatenation with parameterized queries using prepared statements. In Node.js with SQLite, use placeholders (?) for all user input. In the vulnerable query `SELECT * FROM products WHERE name LIKE '%' + userInput + '%'`, change to `SELECT * FROM products WHERE name LIKE ?` and pass `['%' + userInput + '%']` as a separate parameter array. Alternatively, use an ORM (Sequelize, TypeORM, Prisma) that handles parameterization automatically. The key principle is that SQL structure and user data must be submitted to the database engine separately, never combined as a string. Add input validation as defense-in-depth (whitelist allowed characters, limit length), but this is not a substitute for parameterization."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "7.5",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-89",
                  "stride:T"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/89.html"
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-347",
              "name": "JWT Algorithm=None Attack - Complete Authentication Bypass",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "JWT Algorithm=None Attack - Complete Authentication Bypass"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "The application accepts JWT tokens with the `alg: none` algorithm in the Authorization header, allowing attackers to forge valid authentication tokens without any cryptographic key. This enables complete authentication bypass and privilege escalation to any role."
              },
              "defaultConfiguration": {
                "level": "error"
              },
              "help": {
                "text": "The application accepts JWT tokens with the `alg: none` algorithm in the Authorization header, allowing attackers to forge valid authentication tokens without any cryptographic key. This enables complete authentication bypass and privilege escalation to any role.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can forge JWT tokens impersonating any user, including administrators. This allows unauthorized access to all protected resources, user data modification, and complete system compromise. No valid credentials are required—only the ability to send HTTP requests.\n\nImplement strict JWT algorithm enforcement on the server. Configure the JWT verification library to only accept the expected algorithm (e.g., RS256) and reject any token with a different algorithm, particularly the \"none\" algorithm. Verify that all JWT libraries have algorithm pinning enabled. Additionally, implement additional checks to reject tokens before signature verification if the algorithm is not explicitly whitelisted.",
                "markdown": "The application accepts JWT tokens with the `alg: none` algorithm in the Authorization header, allowing attackers to forge valid authentication tokens without any cryptographic key. This enables complete authentication bypass and privilege escalation to any role.\n\nAn unauthenticated attacker can forge JWT tokens impersonating any user, including administrators. This allows unauthorized access to all protected resources, user data modification, and complete system compromise. No valid credentials are required—only the ability to send HTTP requests.\n\nImplement strict JWT algorithm enforcement on the server. Configure the JWT verification library to only accept the expected algorithm (e.g., RS256) and reject any token with a different algorithm, particularly the \"none\" algorithm. Verify that all JWT libraries have algorithm pinning enabled. Additionally, implement additional checks to reject tokens before signature verification if the algorithm is not explicitly whitelisted."
              },
              "properties": {
                "security-severity": "9.8",
                "tags": [
                  "security",
                  "CWE-347",
                  "stride:T",
                  "stride:I"
                ]
              },
              "helpUri": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/347.html"
            },
